>
> many thanks for the two comments. Well, my research needs involves a
> robust estimation of abundance or a robust "abundance" value allowing me
> to compare between two or more habitats.  The main problem was empashized
> in the first e-mail, due to particular biological and ecological
> attributes extensive long-term capture-recapture is needed. This sort of
> work is nowadays impossible.
>
> I will try to explain the one-day fieldwork procedures, that could be
> repeated several times in the same place. First, I select a surveying
> place where two people go to work. There each one survey an independent
> area during about 3-5 hours. Some of the snakes are found active in the
> ground (or maybe thermorregulating), but they are mostly caugth when
> founded under stones. I've both detected/not detected data and the number
> of different individuals seen during the work. The problem is that I'm
> concerned about how to calculate any index because, as an example one day
> we found out 1 snake, but three days later we caugth 7 snakes using the
> same effort. For us, it looks like the animals are hide in the
> subterranean pathways of voles -abundant in our study sites- and then
> remains undetected.
>
> Distance sampling is not a valid approach, since I did not think that
> detectability depends upon distance from oberserver. Further, we did'nt
> make transect, rather we survey the complete areas (i. e., we cover all
> the surface and remove any stone). Other potential method, the
> repeatedcapture-per-unit-effort until we can calculate the regression line
> is unsuitable because reasons as the previously described, with the same
> effort the first day we found 1 snake, but the second day we found 7.
> Therefore, I came to the RN model that it seems to me to be a good
> starting point or a way to calculate an abundance parameter suited to be
> comapred among sites.
>
> Perhaps, can I calculate the detectability in each survey site and compare
> its value? Any prefered procedure if doing this?
>
> Many thanks again,
>
> Pablo
